  "question": {
    "text": "A common emitter amplifier circuit, built using an NPN transistor, is shown in the figure. Its dc current gain is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>250</mn></math>,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"italic\">C</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi>k&#937;</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"italic\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"italic\">C</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math> . The minimum base current for <math><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi></mrow></msub></math> to reach saturation is<br /><br /><img alt=\"\" src=\"assets\\qt0158_f32fbfc45525.jpg\" style=\"width: 250px; height: 193px;\" />"
  },
